  "summary": "New preprints test whether clusters and spatial domains are real and separate spillover, ambient RNA and doublets from genuine cell state.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is calibrated statistical inference for single-cell and spatial transcriptomics, replacing heuristic outputs with tested or explicitly modelled structure. Two preprints formalise partition significance: SigDoTS applies hypothesis testing with FWER control to candidate spatial domains, merging or retaining them via null simulations, and ProClust merges overclustered scRNA-seq groups using log-concave cluster modelling and projection-based testing. A second strand models technical and biological confounders jointly. DisCell generatively separates intrinsic cell state, niche response and neighbour spillover. PRISM uses EM to jointly model ambient RNA, doublets and cell-type assignment. spSheaf infers domains directly from raw transcript molecules via cellular sheaf theory, avoiding segmentation. scCircaSim supplies ground-truth circadian single-cell and spatial data with known phases and rhythm parameters for benchmarking. IsoVita extends the inferential theme to compositional features along continuous covariates, with change point and spline tests for isoform usage.",
